\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The continuum integrability condition}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{In the revised arXiv version gr-qc/0405033v1 of
\citef{lasenby1998gravity}, Section 3.1, equation (3.17), p. 19,
the inverse position-gauge field satisfies a vanishing-curl
condition. This is a cited continuum statement; none of the finite
proofs here uses it. On one fixed finite complex,
\ref{fdip-00OE} uses the vanishing of the cohomology class of the
translation cochain. A gauge with rotational component #{1}
assigns a vector #{X_x} to each vertex and changes the cochain by
the coboundary of #{X}. This changes vector labels attached to
the existing cells; it supplies no permutation of the fixed vertex
set and no continuum coordinate on the complex.}

\p{The reference cochain of \ref{fdip-00OF} fails to be pure
gauge exactly when some period #{N\epsilon_i} is nonzero in
#{V}. In that case no single-valued vertex cochain reproduces the
reference frame. For the affine group of \ref{fdip-00OB},
restricting the rotational element to satisfy #{\alpha(R)=R}
and #{R\tilde R=1} gives the ordinary rotor formula
#{\rho(R)c=Rc\tilde R}. This finite pair construction is
separate from the cited continuum fields. The argument for the
reference cochain uses neither a signature nor an order on #{k}.}
